From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] hnetcfg: the rules programs add reach the firewall at once

Stained Glass Firewall (sg-session's sg-firewall, 2026-10-07) applies the
rules Windows programs store with INetFwPolicy2 (0792 keeps them where
Windows does, in the machine's FirewallRules key). It reads them from the
machine's registry file, which wineserver writes only every half minute:
Sonos's installer added its rule and started Sonos at once, and the
firewall would have asked the person about a program its installer had
just allowed.

A rule stored or removed is now also handed to the firewall at once, as
sg-admind's "firewall registry-push" (an installer is elevated: SYSTEM,
whose spool it is; anyone else's push is refused and the rule waits for
the file). The policy answers from the firewall's status file what it is
really doing: the kinds of network in use (CurrentProfileTypes) and whether
it is on for each (FirewallEnabled); put_FirewallEnabled asks sg-admind
(E_ACCESSDENIED for a program that is not elevated, as on Windows).
Without the firewall the answers are as before (on, Private).

The older interfaces installers still use (INetFwMgr: an authorized
application, a globally open port) kept nothing; each now becomes a rule,
as INetFwPolicy2's (their objects keep their name, port, protocol and
whether they are enabled). include/wine/sgfirewall.h is the way to the
firewall's status and to sg-admind, for hnetcfg and netsh (1502).

Test: test/fwnetsh-gate.sh with test/fwnetsh-v1.vbs (mutant
SG_MUTANT_FW_RULES_NOT_PUSHED); test/fwrules-gate.sh still passes.
